Content licence — Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 4.0 International
==========================================================================

Everything under `content/` in this repository — runbooks, guides,
reference cards, the lexicon and the media files — is licensed under
CC BY-SA 4.0, NOT under the MIT licence that covers the engine in `src/`.

    https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/

Why the split (design doc section 19.2)
---------------------------------------
Runbooks are documentation, not software. ShareAlike means that when
somebody improves a troubleshooting tree — adds the branch you missed,
corrects a step that no longer matches Windows 11 — that improvement
stays available to everyone who relies on it. Operational knowledge gets
better by circulating; the engine gets adopted by being frictionless.
Two licences, two goals.

You are free to:
  Share  — copy and redistribute the material in any medium or format
  Adapt  — remix, transform, and build upon the material for any purpose,
           including commercially

Under the following terms:
  Attribution — credit the helpdesk-guide project and link to the licence.
  ShareAlike  — distribute your contributions under the same licence.

Contributing content
--------------------
By submitting content to `content/`, you agree to license it under
CC BY-SA 4.0, and you confirm that:

  * you wrote it, or it came from a CC BY-SA-compatible source that you
    have credited in the record's `source:` block;
  * it contains no vendor documentation copied verbatim — see section 22.6
    of the design document: cite and link, write the summary yourself;
  * it contains no passwords, tokens, licence keys or internal hostnames.
    The compiler enforces this, but the compiler is the second line of
    defence, not the first.

Site-specific content belongs in `content-local/`, which is git-ignored
and never published.
